# 1Password-backed value resolution — LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY.
#
# NOTE: decorator names must never appear with a leading at-sign in prose here.
# Varlock parses every at-token in a comment block as a decorator, so a sentence
# mentioning one is read as a redeclaration with the sentence as its value.
#
# WHY THIS LIVES IN ITS OWN DIRECTORY
#
# No app schema imports this file, and that is the point. Two things go wrong if
# the plugin is registered in the root schema instead:
#
#   1. An importing app inherits the initOp decorator and fails immediately with
#      "ref(): invalid dependency: OP_TOKEN", because the token is not in its
#      pick list. Verified, not theorised.
#   2. Deployed containers run varlock against a per-app schema. Registering the
#      plugin upstream would pull the 1Password SDK's WASM module into a 512MB
#      container that has no 1Password credentials and no need for them.
#
# HOW VALUES REACH AN APP
#
# direnv loads this file and exports the resolved values into your shell (see
# .envrc). Per-app validation then reads them from process.env, which sits at
# the top of varlock's precedence chain:
#
#   .env.schema < .env < .env.local < .env.[currentEnv] < process.env
#
# That means local and deployed environments share one mechanism: something
# fills process.env — 1Password here, ECS task definitions in dev and prod — and
# the per-app schema validates whatever it finds. The apps never know or care
# which one it was.
#
# SETUP
#
#   1. brew install 1password-cli
#      Desktop-app auth needs the BETA op CLI (v2.33.0+) for `op environment`;
#      the stable build does not have it. A service-account token uses the SDK
#      and has no such requirement.
#   2. 1Password -> Settings -> Developer -> Integrate with 1Password CLI
#   3. Put the environment id in the repo-root .env.local (gitignored). It has to
#      resolve before authentication, so it cannot come from 1Password itself:
#        echo 'OP_DMR_ENVIRONMENT=<environment-id>' >> .env.local
#   4. direnv allow
#
# Check it with:  yarn varlock load --path config/1password --agent
#
# @defaultRequired=infer
# @plugin(@varlock/1password-plugin)
# cacheTtl exists so direnv does not hit 1Password (and prompt for Touch ID) on
# every new shell. Keep it SHORT: the cache stores empty and failed results too,
# not just successful ones. An hour-long TTL turned a transient setup failure
# into an hour of silently undefined values and cost a real debugging session.
# If values look stale or missing, bypass it with --skip-cache before assuming
# anything else is wrong.
# @initOp(token=$OP_TOKEN, allowAppAuth=true, cacheTtl=5m)
# @setValuesBulk(opLoadEnvironment($OP_DMR_ENVIRONMENT))
# @import(../../)
# ---

# 1Password service-account token. Leave empty to authenticate through the
# desktop app instead, which is the normal path for a developer machine. Note
# that desktop auth connects as YOU, with your full access rather than a scoped
# service account — fine for dev secrets, not for anything else.
# @type=opServiceAccountToken @internal
OP_TOKEN=

# Which 1Password environment to bulk-load. Set this in the repo-root .env.local;
# it is read before the plugin authenticates, so it cannot itself come from
# 1Password.
# @internal
OP_DMR_ENVIRONMENT=
